  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes the anatomy of the mitral valve and its surrounding structures from two complementary views. Overall, it shows how the valve sits between the left atrium and left ventricle, how its leaflets are organized, and how nearby cardiac landmarks relate to surgical or anatomical orientation.\n\nOne view looks down on the mitral annulus, showing the large anterior leaflet and the posterior leaflet divided into three scallops, labeled P1 to P3. The annulus is closely related superiorly to the aortic valve annulus and the aorto-mitral curtain, with the anterolateral and posteromedial trigones marking important fibrous points. The circumflex artery and coronary sinus run near the posterior-lateral aspect of the valve, emphasizing how adjacent vessels lie close to the mitral annulus.\n\nThe other view shows the valve in cross-section between the left atrium and left ventricle. Here, the anterior leaflet faces a posterior leaflet subdivided into segments A1–A3 and P1–P3, with a small commissural leaflet between them. Chordae tendinae connect the leaflets to the anterolateral and posteromedial papillary muscles. Together, the figure highlights the segmented leaflet geometry and the subvalvular support apparatus that controls mitral valve function.",
